Cloudberry's singlenode tests on the port: the differences from Cloudberry's
expected output that have been reviewed, and are the port's for a reason of
its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../canon.pl gives it -- the
lines removed and added, hunk by hunk, with no file names, line numbers or
plan lines -- named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a
pass in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test
as passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's, finds
no difference or exactly the file's.

Most of every difference is a statement that fails, for a reason its error
names, and what comes of it: the rows a query over a table that was never
made would have returned, a later statement in the same transaction refused.
The reasons, by what brings them, and then each test:

  Append-optimized and column-oriented tables, their catalogs and the bitmap
  index -- WITH (appendonly=true), orientation, ENCODING, compresstype,
  pg_appendonly, pg_attribute_encoding, USING bitmap, ao_row: M5.

  External tables, gpfdist, COPY ... LOG ERRORS and the error log functions,
  FILL MISSING FIELDS, NEWLINE, ESCAPE outside CSV mode: M5, the loading path.

  gp_dist_random(), gp_segment_id, gp_segment_configuration,
  gp_distribution_policy, gp_configuration_history, and the settings of a
  cluster that dispatches (gp_enable_direct_dispatch,
  test_print_direct_dispatch_info, gp_interconnect_queue_depth,
  gp_motion_cost_per_row, gp_cte_sharing, gp_enable_multiphase_agg,
  test_print_prefetch_joinqual): M2.

  statement_mem and gp_workfile_compression: M6, resource management.

  Cloudberry's classic partition syntax -- PARTITION BY RANGE (c) (START (1)
  END (10) EVERY (1)), ALTER TABLE ... DROP DEFAULT PARTITION -- and its table
  functions, TABLE (...) and anytable: grammar the port's O26 does not have.

  gp_autostats_mode, enable_groupagg and gp_recursive_cte: settings the port
  has no counterpart of.  Recursive CTEs are always on, as in PostgreSQL.

  PL/Python, which the image does not carry, and what Cloudberry's tests make
  with it: count_operator() and count_index_scans(), which count the nodes of
  a plan.

  PostgreSQL 19's own behaviour where Cloudberry changed its parser or
  PostgreSQL has changed since: OVER (w) of a window with a frame ("cannot
  copy window"), a frame offset that reads a column ("argument of ROWS must
  not contain variables"), a chain of UNIONs over NULLs, which PostgreSQL
  types one UNION at a time ("UNION types text and integer cannot be
  matched"), IS NOT DISTINCT FROM printed as NOT ... IS DISTINCT FROM,
  standard_conforming_strings = off and escape_string_warning, which
  PostgreSQL 19 no longer has, NOT NULL constraints, which PostgreSQL 18
  keeps in pg_constraint, and "operator does not exist", which PostgreSQL 19
  explains in a DETAIL and a HINT where PostgreSQL 14 had one HINT.

  DECODE and CASE x WHEN IS NOT DISTINCT FROM y, which Cloudberry's parser
  makes a CASE with an operand and the port's rewrite a searched CASE with
  the operand in each arm, because PostgreSQL 19 compares a CASE's operand
  with = and nothing else: a view prints the searched CASE, and the operand
  is evaluated in each arm the CASE tries rather than once.  The answers are
  Cloudberry's, and so are the errors and where they are reported.

The tests

bfv_aggregate  count_operator(), gp_enable_multiphase_agg, an
  append-optimized table, PL/Python, OVER (w) with a frame, a frame offset
  that reads a column.
bfv_catalog  count_operator(), pg_stat_last_operation (Cloudberry's catalog of
  when an object was last changed), escape_string_warning and
  standard_conforming_strings = off, PL/Python, and one row more in a query
  over pg_constraint: the table's NOT NULL constraint.
bfv_dd, bfv_dd_multicolumn, bfv_dd_types  direct dispatch's test setting,
  gp_autostats_mode, the classic partition syntax, the bitmap index.
bfv_index  count_index_scans(), append-optimized tables, the classic
  partition syntax.
bfv_olap  gp_motion_cost_per_row, OVER (w) with a frame.
case_gp  The deparse of IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, and of the CASE form, which
  prints as the searched CASE it was rewritten into; blip(1), the operand of
  a CASE with three arms, called three times where Cloudberry calls it once,
  so calls_to_blip has seven rows where it has five and the CASE's answer is
  the same 666; and PostgreSQL 19's DETAIL for "operator does not exist",
  twice.
cluster_gp  an append-optimized table.
create_table_like_gp  external tables, append-optimized tables and their
  catalogs, ENCODING, gp_default_storage_options, ao_row.
gp_gin_index  the complex type, which Cloudberry's own earlier tests make;
  and the index wowidx and the rows of test_tsvector that PostgreSQL 19's
  tsearch test, which runs before it here, leaves behind (two counts one and
  three higher).
gp_recursive_cte  gp_recursive_cte, whose "off" Cloudberry's test expects to
  refuse a recursive CTE, and a plan gpdiff.pl does not take for one, which is
  PostgreSQL 19's planner's.
gpcopy  COPY's LOG ERRORS, FILL MISSING FIELDS, NEWLINE and ESCAPE outside
  CSV mode, the error log functions, gp_configuration_history,
  gp_autostats_mode, and the broken_int4 type of Cloudberry's regress.so.
indexjoin  an append-optimized table.
join_gp  gp_dist_random(), the classic partition syntax, statement_mem,
  gp_workfile_compression, test_print_prefetch_joinqual.
limit_gp  statement_mem.
qp_union_intersect  gp_enable_direct_dispatch, set inside a transaction the
  rest of which is then refused.
qp_with_clause  gp_cte_sharing, append-optimized and column-oriented tables,
  the bitmap index, enable_groupagg.
returning_gp  the classic partition syntax, an append-optimized table.
singlenode_compatibility_cbdb  the warning Cloudberry's single-node mode
  gives for DISTRIBUTED BY (the port records the policy, for ORCA and M2),
  append-optimized tables, TABLE (...) and anytable, and ALTER TABLE ... SET
  DISTRIBUTED BY, which Cloudberry refuses in single-node mode and the port's
  grammar does not have.
subselect_gp  the classic partition syntax, gp_segment_id,
  gp_distribution_policy, gp_interconnect_queue_depth, append-optimized
  tables.
subselect_gp2  an external web table.
tag  Tag definitions are the database's here, in gp_sql.tag, and Cloudberry's
  are the cluster's, in the shared catalog pg_tag (cloudberry.md, "Still
  open"): the test makes its tags in one database and uses them from postgres
  and other_db, where the port has none.  Its queries of pg_tag and
  pg_tag_description find no such catalogs.  CREATE TAG, ALTER TAG and DROP
  TAG are CALLs of gp_sql's procedures, and an error from one is Cloudberry's
  message, with a CONTEXT naming gp_sql's functions.  Where gp_sql is not
  there -- in the two databases the tags are not defined in -- a TAG clause is
  refused before its statement runs, "tags need the gp_sql extension in this
  database", and ALTER SCHEMA ... TAG, which is a CALL, finds no schema
  gp_sql, reported at the start of the statement.  A malformed TAG clause
  (TAG; or TAG ()) is PostgreSQL's error at the word the rewrite left -- a
  syntax error, or "unrecognized role option" of a role's -- where
  Cloudberry's syntax error is at the next.
union_gp  count_operator(), gp_distribution_policy, an external table, PL/
  Python, and a chain of UNIONs over NULLs.
update_gp  gp_segment_id, gp_segment_configuration, append-optimized tables,
  the classic partition syntax, gp_autostats_mode.
